				\begin{document}$$h_{i} ^{(1)}{^{\prime}} = \sigma \left({\sum\limits_{{j \in N_{i} }} {\alpha_{ij} Wh_{j} } }\right)$$\end{document}hi(1)′=σ∑j∈NiαijWhj\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${h}_{i}^{(1)}{^{\prime}}$$\end{document}hi(1)′ is the new feature output by GATv2 for each node i, and σ is the activation function. To further improve the expression ability of the model, the attention mechanism is expanded to a multi-head attention mechanism.6\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$h_{i}^{(1)}{^{\prime\prime}} = \sigma \left( {\frac{1}{K}\sum\limits_{k = 1}^{K} {\sum\limits_{{j \in N_{i} }} {\alpha_{ij}^{\left( k \right)} W^{\left( k \right)} h_{j}^{\left( 0 \right)} } } } \right)$$\end{document}hi(1)″=σ1K∑k=1K∑j∈NiαijkWkhj0where K is the number of attention heads, αij(k) is the attention coefficient of the k-th head, and W(k) is the weight matrix of the k-th head.

In this paper, we convert each amino acid into a numerical value, representing the protein as a sequence of integers. We apply an embedding layer to this sequence, where each amino acid is represented by a 128-dimensional vector. For training, the sequences are standardized to a fixed length of 1000; shorter sequences are padded with zeros to reach this length. First, CNN is used to initially extract protein features, and pooling operations are used to improve the generalization ability of the model. Using P to represent the protein features extracted by the CNN network and a bidirectional long short-term memory network (Bi-LSTM) is used to further extract features:7\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\overrightarrow {{h_{t} }} = LSTM_{forward} \left( {P_{t} ,h_{t - 1} } \right)$$\end{document}ht→=LSTMforwardPt,ht-18\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\overleftarrow {{h_{t} }} = LSTM_{backward} \left( {P_{t} ,h_{t + 1} } \right)$$\end{document}ht←=LSTMbackwardPt,ht+19\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$h_{t} = \left[ {\overrightarrow {{h_{t} }} ;\overleftarrow {{h_{t} }} } \right]$$\end{document}ht=ht→;ht←ht-1 and ht+1 represent the hidden state at time steps t-1 and t + 1 respectively. H = [h1, h2, h3, …, hn], H represents the vector composition of Bi-LSTM module output.

Based on the features extracted by Bi-LSTM, we adopt Transformer to dynamically assign different weights to each feature through its attention mechanism, allowing the model to automatically focus on the most informative parts of the sequence, thereby further extracting useful features:10\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$Z = LayerNorm\left( {H + MultiHeadSelfAttention\left( H \right) + FeedForward\left( H \right)} \right)$$\end{document}Z=LayerNormH+MultiHeadSelfAttentionH+FeedForwardH11\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$MultiHeadSelfAttention\left( H \right) = Concat\left( {head_{1} ,head_{2} ,...,head_{h} } \right) \cdot W^{O}$$\end{document}MultiHeadSelfAttentionH=Concathead1,head2,...,headh·WO

The Calculation of Query, Key, and Value:12\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$Query^{\left( i \right)} = H \cdot W^{Q} ,Key^{\left( i \right)} = H \cdot W^{K} ,Value^{\left( i \right)} = H \cdot W^{V}$$\end{document}Queryi=H·WQ,Keyi=H·WK,Valuei=H·WV

The Calculation of attention weight:13\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$Attention^{\left( i \right)} = Softmax\left( {\frac{{Query^{\left( i \right)} \cdot \left( {Key^{\left( i \right)} } \right)^{T} }}{{\sqrt {d_{k} } }}} \right) \cdot Value^{\left( i \right)}$$\end{document}Attentioni=SoftmaxQueryi·KeyiTdk·Valuei

Merger of multiple heads’ attention:14\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
