				\begin{document}$$Z^{\left( l \right)} = LayerNorm\left( {Concat\left( {head_{1} ,head_{2} ,...,head_{h} } \right) \cdot W^{O} + H} \right)$$\end{document}Zl=LayerNormConcathead1,head2,...,headh·WO+H

The above-learned drug vector and protein vector are connected and sent to the fully connected layer to obtain the final DTA prediction value y:16\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$y = W_{output} \left[ {D,P} \right] + b_{output}$$\end{document}y=WoutputD,P+boutputwhere Woutput represents the weight matrix of the fully connected layer, boutput represent the bias of the fully connected layer.

The mean-square error (MSE) is a measure that reflects the degree of difference between the model’s predicted value and the true value. The smaller the value, the better. The calculation method is detailed in Eq. 17.17\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$MSE = \frac{1}{n}\sum\limits_{i = 1}^{n} {\left( {\mathop {y_{i} }\limits^{ \wedge } - y_{i} } \right)}^{2}$$\end{document}MSE=1n∑i=1nyi∧-yi2

The concordance index (CI) evaluates the discriminative performance of different models. The CI value closer to 1 indicates a better fit of the model. Equation 18 shows the calculation method.18\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$CI = \frac{1}{Z}\sum\limits_{{\sigma_{i} > \sigma_{j} }} {\varphi \left( {b_{i} - b_{j} } \right)}$$\end{document}CI=1Z∑σi>σjφbi-bj

The rm2 index is utilized in DeepGLSTM, serves as an evaluation metric for regression tasks. Equation 19 outlines the calculation procedure.19\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$r_{m}^{2} = r^{2} \times \left( {1 - \sqrt {r^{2} - r_{0}^{2} } } \right)$$\end{document}rm2=r2×1-r2-r02Where the squares of the correlation coefficients with and without the intercept are denoted as r2 and r02, respectively.
